      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Still reproducible as of October 2026.\n\nI tested with an OSCQuery service whose tree advertises only two specific endpoints under /avatar. VRChat still sent every avatar parameter, almost none of them for the advertised endpoints. Advertising the whole /avatar node instead made no difference, so the advertised tree seems to be used only to decide whether to send at all, not what to send.\n\nFor apps that sit idle in the background most of the time, this is a constant wake-up and decode cost for data that is thrown away. The amount grows with the avatar's parameter count, and every OSCQuery app the user runs pays it separately.\n\nEither filtering by the advertised endpoints, or supporting the OSCQuery LISTEN extension (HOST_INFO currently doesn't list it), would solve this. Thanks!",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2024-03-30T19:05:25.903Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "OscQuery requires each node is capable of responding HTTP requests pertaining to the OSC methods that it's able to read/write to/from. VRChat *should* then only send to these addresses due to it now knowing what addresses the node needs. Despite this, VRChat sends all values to the client over OSC anyway. This has been an issue since OSCQuery's launch.\n\nFor example, I only need to receive information about avatar changes (/avatar/change), and my HTTP server responds with that single address as a read/write string value, yet it sends all values anyway, and I need to parse every single one of them to find the correct one.\n\nFixing this would improve performance both on VRChat's side not having to encode all that unused OSC data, and on the app's side only having to decode the relevant data to find the data it actually needs.",
